---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Research - Solo Ghost" description="Research directions in wireless PHY, channel structure, mobility, adaptive estimation and tracking, receiver design and AI-native communication systems.">
  <section class="research-hero">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">RESEARCH</p>

      <h1>Understanding structure before optimizing the receiver.</h1>

      <p class="lead">
        Solo Ghost studies how physical and statistical structure in wireless
        signals can be identified, modeled and exploited to build more
        adaptive communication systems.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="thesis-section">
    <div class="container two-column">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">RESEARCH THESIS</p>
        <h2>Wireless signals are not arbitrary data.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Wireless channels evolve according to physical processes. They contain
          spatial, temporal, frequency and delay structure that can change with
          mobility, propagation conditions and interference.
        </p>

        <p>
          Solo Ghost investigates how that structure can be understood and used
          by receiver algorithms rather than treating every observation as an
          unrelated realization.
        </p>

        <p>
          The goal is not to replace classical signal processing, but to discover
          where structure-aware methods can make receivers more adaptive,
          efficient and robust.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="trajectory-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-label">RESEARCH TRAJECTORY</p>

      <h2>From wireless signal understanding toward AI-native receivers.</h2>

      <div class="trajectory">
        <article class="trajectory-item">
          <span class="number">01</span>
          <h3>Wireless PHY &amp; Signal Processing</h3>
          <p>
            Discrete-time signals, OFDM, synchronization, CFO, channel
            estimation, equalization, detection and MIMO form the engineering
            foundation.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="trajectory-item">
          <span class="number">02</span>
          <h3>Channel Structure</h3>
          <p>
            Investigating spatial, temporal, frequency and delay-related
            structure in wireless channels and how that structure changes
            across observations.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="trajectory-item">
          <span class="number">03</span>
          <h3>Mobility &amp; Doppler</h3>
          <p>
            Understanding channel evolution, temporal coherence and the
            conditions under which conventional correlation and tracking
            assumptions become unreliable.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="trajectory-item">
          <span class="number">04</span>
          <h3>Structure-Aware Receivers</h3>
          <p>
            Exploring adaptive channel acquisition, estimation, equalization
            and detection methods that exploit identifiable signal or channel
            structure.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="trajectory-item">
          <span class="number">05</span>
          <h3>Adaptive Estimation &amp; Tracking</h3>
          <p>
            Studying evolving wireless channels as dynamic states and exploring
            recursive, predictive and structure-aware approaches for estimating
            and tracking them over time.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="trajectory-item">
          <span class="number">06</span>
          <h3>AI-Native Communication Systems</h3>
          <p>
            A longer-term direction in which learned models work alongside
            physics-based signal processing to create receivers that learn
            from structured wireless observations.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="areas-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="section-label">CURRENT RESEARCH AREAS</p>
          <h2>Problems we are actively exploring.</h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="areas-grid">
        <article class="area-card">
          <span class="area-number">01</span>
          <h3>Channel Acquisition &amp; Estimation</h3>
          <p>
            How much channel information is actually necessary, and how should
            acquisition and estimation adapt to channel structure and evolution?
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="area-card">
          <span class="area-number">02</span>
          <h3>Mobility-Aware Processing</h3>
          <p>
            How should receivers behave when Doppler and rapid channel evolution
            challenge assumptions built around slow or stationary channels?
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="area-card">
          <span class="area-number">03</span>
          <h3>Adaptive Estimation &amp; Tracking</h3>
          <p>
            How can recursive estimation, state representations and temporal
            models help receivers predict and track evolving channel conditions?
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="area-card">
          <span class="area-number">04</span>
          <h3>Structure-Aware Equalization</h3>
          <p>
            How can delay, frequency and channel structure inform more efficient
            representations and receiver processing?
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="area-card">
          <span class="area-number">05</span>
          <h3>Adaptive Signal Detection</h3>
          <p>
            How can detection complexity and signal representation adapt to the
            structure and conditions actually present in the received signal?
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="area-card">
          <span class="area-number">06</span>
          <h3>Wireless Simulation &amp; Dataset Generation</h3>
          <p>
            Building physics-accurate simulation workflows that can generate
            controlled wireless observations for research, benchmarking and
            future learning-based receiver development.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="area-card">
          <span class="area-number">07</span>
          <h3>AI-Native PHY</h3>
          <p>
            Investigating how machine learning can augment, rather than obscure,
            the physical and statistical structure already present in wireless
            signals.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="methods-section">
    <div class="container two-column">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">TECHNICAL METHODS</p>
        <h2>Models and tools used to study evolving wireless systems.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="method-list">
        <div class="method-item">
          <h3>Recursive Estimation</h3>
          <p>
            Updating channel knowledge sequentially as new observations arrive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="method-item">
          <h3>State-Space Modeling</h3>
          <p>
            Representing evolving channel behavior through latent states,
            observations and temporal dynamics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="method-item">
          <h3>Kalman &amp; Kalman-Like Tracking</h3>
          <p>
            Studying prediction and measurement updates for time-varying
            channel estimation and tracking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="method-item">
          <h3>Temporal &amp; Statistical Models</h3>
          <p>
            Using correlation, coherence and stochastic channel models to
            understand how wireless observations evolve over time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="method-item">
          <h3>Structure-Aware Representations</h3>
          <p>
            Looking for lower-dimensional, sparse, subspace or otherwise
            structured representations that preserve useful receiver information.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="method-item">
          <h3>Physics-Accurate Simulation</h3>
          <p>
            Creating controlled environments for testing receiver assumptions,
            generating observations and benchmarking adaptive methods.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mobility-section">
    <div class="container two-column">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">DYNAMIC WIRELESS SYSTEMS</p>
        <h2>Mobility changes the assumptions.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A recurring research question at Solo Ghost is what happens when
          receiver assumptions developed for relatively stable channels are
          exposed to mobility and Doppler.
        </p>

        <p>
          This motivates work across channel correlation, coherence, recursive
          estimation, state tracking and adaptive receiver behavior.
        </p>

        <a class="text-link" href="/notes/">Explore technical notes -&gt;</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="applications-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-label">POTENTIAL APPLICATION DOMAINS</p>

      <h2>Research that can extend across demanding wireless environments.</h2>

      <div class="application-grid">
        <article>
          <h3>UAV / Aerial Communications</h3>
          <p>High mobility, rapidly changing channels and Doppler.</p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>NTN / Satellite</h3>
          <p>Mobility, Doppler, long propagation paths and constrained resources.</p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>5G / 6G / Private Networks</h3>
          <p>Adaptive receiver processing for evolving network conditions.</p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Radar / Sensing</h3>
          <p>Motion, Doppler, structure and limited measurements.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="application-note">
        These are research directions and potential application domains,
        not representations of current products or deployed systems.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="evidence-section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="section-label">RESEARCH EVIDENCE</p>

      <h2>Research is accompanied by executable engineering work.</h2>

      <p>
        Solo Ghost documents experiments through technical notes, reproducible
        simulations and engineering artifacts, while selected work is reflected
        in the public IP portfolio.
      </p>

      <div class="evidence-links">
        <a href="/artifacts/">Engineering Artifacts</a>
        <a href="/notes/">Technical Notes</a>
        <a href="/ip/">IP Portfolio</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/haripriyap/doppler-coherence-analysis" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .research-hero {
    padding: 126px 0 112px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .eyebrow,
  .section-label,
  .number,
  .area-number {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  h1 {
    max-width: 920px;
    margin: 20px 0 30px;
    font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 6vw, 5.4rem);
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.055em;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .lead {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .thesis-section,
  .trajectory-section,
  .areas-section,
  .methods-section,
  .mobility-section,
  .applications-section,
  .evidence-section {
    padding: 112px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .two-column {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(260px, 0.8fr) minmax(0, 1.2fr);
    gap: 80px;
  }

  h2 {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 14px 0 28px;
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3vw, 2.8rem);
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
    font-weight: 560;
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 700px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .prose p {
    margin: 0 0 22px;
  }

  .trajectory,
  .areas-grid {
    margin-top: 48px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--line);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .trajectory-item {
    min-height: 270px;
    padding: 34px;
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .number {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 56px;
  }

  .trajectory-item h3,
  .area-card h3,
  .application-grid h3,
  .method-item h3 {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }

  .trajectory-item p,
  .area-card p,
  .application-grid p,
  .method-item p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 520px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .area-card {
    min-height: 240px;
    padding: 34px;
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .area-number {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 46px;
  }

  .method-list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--line);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .method-item {
    min-height: 170px;
    padding: 28px;
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .method-item h3 {
    font-size: 1.12rem;
  }

  .mobility-section {
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .application-grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
  }

  .application-grid article {
    min-height: 210px;
    padding: 28px;
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  .application-grid h3 {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

  .application-note {
    margin: 22px 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .evidence-section {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .evidence-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 18px 30px;
    margin-top: 36px;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .text-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .two-column {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 36px;
    }

    .application-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .research-hero {
      padding: 86px 0 78px;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 13vw, 4rem);
    }

    .thesis-section,
    .trajectory-section,
    .areas-section,
    .methods-section,
    .mobility-section,
    .applications-section,
    .evidence-section {
      padding: 78px 0;
    }

    .trajectory,
    .areas-grid,
    .method-list,
    .application-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .trajectory-item,
    .area-card,
    .application-grid article,
    .method-item {
      min-height: 0;
    }

    .number,
    .area-number {
      margin-bottom: 34px;
    }
  }
</style>
